  <text x="28" y="305" font-size="8.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">LOGARITHMIC MULTIPLEXER DECOMPOSITION: 5 GATES FOR ANY 0..31 SHIFT</text>
  <text x="28" y="322" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">&#8226; Any integer 0 &#8804; shamt &#8804; 31 is uniquely represented as the sum of powers of two (16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1).</text>
  <text x="28" y="336" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">&#8226; When a bit is 1, the multiplexer stage shifts by 2^k; when 0, it passes through untouched. Total latency is always 1 cycle!</text>
  <text x="28" y="350" font-size="7.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000">&#8226; Contrast with shift registers: Shifting 31 bits sequentially would stall the CPU pipeline for 31 full clock cycles!</text>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- BOTTOM INSTRUMENT RACK (TIMING & ARCHITECTURE)        -->
  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <rect x="8" y="376" width="744" height="96" rx="6" fill="#f6f0dc" stroke="#151b20" stroke-width="1.5"/>

  <!-- Module 1: Critical Path Timing -->
  <text x="22" y="394" font-size="8.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">PROPAGATION DELAY</text>
  <text x="22" y="412" font-size="11" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">5 MUX GATE DELAYS (&#8776; 110 ps)</text>
  <text x="22" y="428" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="900">Constant latency regardless of shift amount:</text>
  <text x="22" y="442" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="900">Shifting 1 bit or 31 bits takes exactly 5 gate steps.</text>

  <!-- Module 2: Silicon Area Footprint -->
  <text x="270" y="394" font-size="8.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">SILICON AREA FOOTPRINT</text>
  <text x="270" y="412" font-size="11" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">160 2:1 MUXES (&#8776; 320 GE)</text>
  <text x="270" y="428" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="900">32 bits &#215; 5 stages = 160 multiplexers.</text>
  <text x="270" y="442" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="900">Takes ~15% of an integer execution ALU area.</text>

  <!-- Module 3: Microarchitectural Choice -->
  <text x="515" y="394" font-size="8.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">SCORE CORE ARCHETYPE REALITY</text>
  <text x="515" y="412" font-size="11" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">SERV vs. Ibex / CVA6 / BOOM</text>
  <text x="515" y="428" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="900">&#8226; SERV: Sequential 1-bit shift (30 GE, 1-31 cycles)</text>
  <text x="515" y="442" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-family="sans-serif" font-weight="900">&#8226; Ibex/CVA6/BOOM: Full barrel shifter for 1-cycle IPC</text>
</svg>
            </div>

            <div class="schematic-caption">
              <strong>Figure 2.3:</strong> Interactive 5-stage Logarithmic Barrel Shifter. By decomposing a 0..31 bit shift into powers of two (16, 8, 4, 2, 1), the datapath performs any arbitrary shift in exactly 5 multiplexer delays instead of 31 sequential cycles.
            </div>
          </div>
          <!-- THE PROBLEM & DESIGN TRADE-OFF: BARREL SHIFTER -->
          <div class="tradeoff-box">
            <div class="tradeoff-badge">THE PROBLEM &amp; DESIGN TRADE-OFF</div>
            <div class="tradeoff-grid">
              <div class="tradeoff-col">
                <span class="to-label">&#9889; The Engineering Problem</span>
                <p>
                  Programs constantly perform variable shifts (e.g. pointer arithmetic, bitfields, cryptography). Executing a 31-bit shift one bit per clock cycle would stall the processor for 31 cycles!
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="tradeoff-col">
                <span class="to-label">&#9878; The Silicon Trade-off</span>
                <p>
                  <strong>Single-Cycle Barrel Shifter vs. Sequential Shift Register:</strong><br/>
                  A full 32-bit logarithmic barrel shifter requires 32 &times; 5 = 160 2:1 multiplexers (~320 GE), taking up significant silicon in tiny microcontrollers.<br/>
                  A sequential shift register takes almost zero extra area (~30 GE), but incurs a variable 1-to-31 cycle execution latency.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

