    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- SECTION 3: RISC-V ISA & SINGLE-CYCLE DATAPATH -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section id="isa-datapath" class="fund-section-card">
      <div class="fund-header">
        <span class="fund-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <span class="badge badge-accent">INSTRUCTION ARCHITECTURE</span>
          <h2 class="fund-title">The RISC-V Instruction Set Architecture (ISA)</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fund-lead">
        An <strong>Instruction Set Architecture (ISA)</strong> is the formal contract between software and hardware. It defines the registers, memory addressing rules, and exact binary machine code formats that the microprocessor executes.
      </p>

      <!-- Top-Down Progression: ISA Principles & Registers -->
      <div class="concept-stream">
        <!-- 3.1 LOAD STORE PRINCIPLE -->
        <div class="concept-step-card">
          <div class="step-card-header">
            <span class="step-tag">3.1 DESIGN PHILOSOPHY</span>
            <h3 class="step-card-title">The Load-Store Architecture Principle (RISC vs. CISC)</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            Unlike complex legacy ISAs (such as x86) where single instructions can directly modify DRAM memory, RISC-V is a strict <strong>Load-Store Architecture</strong>:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li>Arithmetic instructions (<code>add</code>, <code>sub</code>, <code>xor</code>) can <em>only</em> operate on data already held inside processor registers.</li>
            <li>Memory access is restricted exclusively to explicit load (<code>lw</code>, <code>ld</code>) and store (<code>sw</code>, <code>sd</code>) instructions.</li>
            <li>This decouples variable memory latency from arithmetic pipeline execution, keeping execution units simple and blistering fast.</li>
          </ul>

          <!-- SCHEMATIC: CISC VS RISC-V EXECUTION -->
          <div class="schematic-container">
            <div class="schematic-header">
              <span class="schematic-tag">ARCHITECTURAL CONTRAST</span>
              <span class="schematic-title">CISC Memory-to-Memory vs. RISC-V Decoupled Load-Store Execution</span>
            </div>
            <div class="schematic-svg-wrap" style="background: var(--cream, #fffaf0); padding: 0.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1.5px solid var(--ink, #151b20);">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 760 330" class="schematic-svg" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <!-- Status Banner -->
  <rect x="2" y="2" width="756" height="24" fill="#f6f0dc" stroke="#151b20" stroke-width="1.5" rx="5"/>
  <circle cx="16" cy="14" r="4" fill="#0d9488"/>
  <text x="26" y="17" font-size="9" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">SCORE ARCHITECTURAL COMPARISON • CISC MEMORY-TO-MEMORY vs. RISC-V LOAD-STORE DECOUPLING</text>

  <!-- Left: CISC Complex Execution -->
  <rect x="10" y="32" width="365" height="288" rx="6" fill="#fffaf0" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="2"/>
  <rect x="10" y="32" width="365" height="32" rx="5" fill="#ffd8ce" stroke="#ea580c" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="22" y="52" font-size="10.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">CISC: ADD [EAX + 0x10], EBX</text>
  <text x="22" y="78" font-size="8.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000">Single complex microcoded instruction (x86)</text>

  <!-- CISC Execution Timeline -->
  <rect x="22" y="90" width="341" height="34" rx="4" fill="#ffd8ce" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="1"/>
  <text x="32" y="105" font-size="8.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000">1. Address Generation: Add EAX + 0x10</text>
  <text x="32" y="117" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">Calculates memory target address</text>

  <!-- Dramatic Memory Wait State -->
  <rect x="22" y="132" width="341" height="48" rx="4" fill="#ffd8ce" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="32" y="150" font-size="9" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">&#9888; 2. DRAM / CACHE READ (50ns WAIT STATE)</text>
  <text x="32" y="163" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">ENTIRE ALU PIPELINE FROZEN: Execution stage stalls!</text>
  <text x="32" y="174" font-size="7" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">Cannot dispatch subsequent independent instructions.</text>

  <rect x="22" y="188" width="341" height="34" rx="4" fill="#ffd8ce" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="1"/>
  <text x="32" y="203" font-size="8.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000">3. ALU Execution: [Mem] + EBX</text>
  <text x="32" y="215" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">Addition finally executes after long memory wait</text>

  <rect x="22" y="230" width="341" height="34" rx="4" fill="#ffd8ce" stroke="#f38469" stroke-width="1"/>
  <text x="32" y="245" font-size="8.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000">4. Memory Writeback: Store to [EAX + 0x10]</text>
  <text x="32" y="257" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">Second memory bus transaction freezes pipeline again</text>

  <text x="22" y="285" font-size="8" font-weight="900" fill="#000000">&#8226; Pipeline Impact: Variable 1..50+ cycle stall; out-of-order execution nightmare.</text>
  <text x="22" y="300" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">&#8226; Decoder Overhead: Requires heavy microcode ROM and complex multi-byte decoding.</text>

  <!-- Right: RISC-V Load-Store Decoupling -->
  <rect x="385" y="32" width="365" height="288" rx="6" fill="#fffaf0" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="2"/>
  <rect x="385" y="32" width="365" height="32" rx="5" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#65a30d" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="397" y="52" font-size="10.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">RISC-V: DECOUPLED LOAD-STORE PIPELINE</text>
  <text x="397" y="78" font-size="8.5" font-weight="900" fill="#000000">Three clean, uniform, single-cycle RISC instructions</text>

  <!-- RISC-V Step 1: lw -->
  <rect x="397" y="90" width="341" height="44" rx="4" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1"/>
  <text x="407" y="106" font-size="9" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">lw  t0, 16(a0)</text>
  <text x="407" y="118" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">&#8226; Explicit Load: Only loads data from memory into register t0.</text>
  <text x="407" y="129" font-size="7" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">Non-blocking in modern cores (scoreboard / out-of-order tracking).</text>

  <!-- RISC-V Step 2: add -->
  <rect x="397" y="142" width="341" height="44" rx="4" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1"/>
  <text x="407" y="158" font-size="9" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">add t0, t0, a1</text>
  <text x="407" y="170" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">&#8226; Pure Register-Register Math: Operates exclusively inside core.</text>
  <text x="407" y="181" font-size="7" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">Strict single-cycle execution: Finishes in &lt; 0.5 ns in ALU!</text>

  <!-- RISC-V Step 3: sw -->
  <rect x="397" y="194" width="341" height="44" rx="4" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1"/>
  <text x="407" y="210" font-size="9" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">sw  t0, 16(a0)</text>
  <text x="407" y="222" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">&#8226; Explicit Store: Buffered in Write Buffer or Store Queue.</text>
  <text x="407" y="233" font-size="7" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">ALU pipeline continues running next instruction without waiting!</text>

  <!-- RISC-V Advantage Highlights -->
  <rect x="397" y="246" width="341" height="64" rx="4" fill="#e7f4c8" stroke="#b7df69" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="407" y="262" font-size="8" font-weight="900" fill="#000000" font-family="monospace">&#10004; ZERO MEMORY STALLS ON ARITHMETIC PIPELINE</text>
  <text x="407" y="276" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">&#8226; Arithmetic execution never touches external memory buses.</text>
  <text x="407" y="289" font-size="7.5" fill="#000000" font-weight="900">&#8226; 32-bit fixed instruction format decodes in less than 1 gate delay!</text>
</svg>
            </div>
            <div class="schematic-caption">
              <strong>Figure 3.1:</strong> Comparison of CISC memory-to-memory instruction execution vs. RISC-V load-store decoupling. In CISC, variable memory wait states stall the entire ALU pipeline. In RISC-V, arithmetic operations are strictly segregated into uniform, single-cycle register steps.
            </div>
          </div>
          <!-- THE PROBLEM & DESIGN TRADE-OFF: RISC VS CISC -->
          <div class="tradeoff-box">
            <div class="tradeoff-badge">THE PROBLEM &amp; DESIGN TRADE-OFF</div>
            <div class="tradeoff-grid">
              <div class="tradeoff-col">
                <span class="to-label">&#9889; The Engineering Problem</span>
                <p>
                  DRAM access time is unpredictable (cache hit = 1ns, cache miss = 80ns). If arithmetic instructions could read/write memory directly, hardware pipelines would constantly stall, making pipelining and out-of-order execution nearly impossible to implement efficiently.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="tradeoff-col">
                <span class="to-label">&#9878; The Silicon Trade-off</span>
                <p>
                  <strong>Code Density vs. Decoder Simplicity:</strong> CISC architectures (x86) achieve 15% to 25% denser compiled binary code by packing memory addresses into arithmetic opcodes. However, decoding variable-length instructions (1 to 15 bytes) requires complex, power-hungry decoders taking &gt;15% of the core area. RISC-V uses fixed 32-bit instructions that decode in less than 1 gate delay!
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 3.2 REGISTER FILE & FORMATS -->
        <div class="concept-step-card">
          <div class="step-card-header">
            <span class="step-tag">3.2 REGISTER FILE</span>
            <h3 class="step-card-title">The 32 General-Purpose Registers (x0–x31) &amp; 2R1W Register File</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            Standard 32-bit RISC-V (RV32I) provides 32 registers, each 32 bits wide. By architectural rule:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><code>x0 (zero)</code> is <strong>hardwired to constant zero</strong>. Writing to <code>x0</code> has no effect; reading it always returns <code>0</code>. This elegantly synthesizes instructions like <code>nop</code> (implemented as <code>addi x0, x0, 0</code>) without wasting opcode space!</li>
            <li><code>x1 (ra)</code>: Return address for function calls.</li>
            <li><code>x2 (sp)</code>: Stack pointer managing memory frames.</li>
            <li><code>x10-x17 (a0-a7)</code>: Function arguments and return values.</li>
          </ul>

