    <!-- SECTION 5: MEMORY HIERARCHY, MMU & OUT-OF-ORDER (APPLICATION CORES BRIDGE) -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section id="memory-virtual" class="fund-section-card">
      <div class="fund-header">
        <span class="fund-num">05</span>
        <div>
          <a href="/app-cores/" style="text-decoration: none;"><span class="badge badge-accent">APPLICATION PROCESSORS &bull; APPLICATION CLASS &rarr;</span></a>
          <h2 class="fund-title">The Memory Hierarchy, MMU &amp; Out-of-Order Execution</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fund-lead">
        While processors operate in gigahertz (&lt;1 nanosecond), external DRAM memory takes 50 to 100 nanoseconds to respond. This is the <strong>Memory Wall</strong>. 64-Bit Application Cores solve this through multi-level caches, hardware virtual memory (MMU), and Out-of-Order (OoO) superscalar execution.
      </p>

      <!-- Top-Down Progression: Virtual Memory & OoO Cores -->
      <div class="concept-stream">
        <!-- 5.1 VIRTUAL MEMORY & SV39 MMU -->
        <div class="concept-step-card">
          <div class="step-card-header">
            <span class="step-tag">5.1 VIRTUAL MEMORY</span>
            <h3 class="step-card-title">Virtual Memory &amp; The Sv39 MMU (3-Level Page Walk)</h3>
          </div>
          <p>
            An operating system like Linux cannot give user programs direct physical access to DRAM (programs would corrupt each other's memory). The <strong>Memory Management Unit (MMU)</strong> translates program <em>virtual addresses</em> into real <em>physical addresses</em>:
          </p>
          <ul class="concept-list">
            <li><strong>Sv39 Standard:</strong> 39-bit virtual addresses mapped via a 3-level radix tree (Page Table Walk: VPN2 &rarr; VPN1 &rarr; VPN0) with 4 KiB memory pages.</li>
            <li><strong>TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer):</strong> An ultra-fast hardware cache that stores recent page translations, avoiding the multi-cycle DRAM page walk on every load and store.</li>
          </ul>

<!-- SCHEMATIC: SV39 PAGE WALK ALL-IN-ONE INTERACTIVE WORKBENCH -->
          <div class="sub-interactive-card" id="ptwWorkbench">
            <div class="sub-widget-header">
              <span class="sub-w-tag">ALL-IN-ONE INTERACTIVE EDA BENCH</span>
              <h4 class="sub-w-title">Virtual Memory Translation — Interactive RISC-V Sv39 Page Table Walker &amp; TLB Simulator</h4>
              <p class="sub-w-desc">
                Toggle between <strong>TLB Hit (Fast 1-Cycle Translation)</strong> and <strong>TLB Miss (3-Level DRAM Page Table Walk)</strong>. Explore how a 39-bit Virtual Address is partitioned into 3 levels of 9-bit page directory indices (VPN2, VPN1, VPN0) and a 12-bit offset (4 KiB page) to locate the 56-bit Physical Address.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="schematic-svg-wrap" style="background: var(--cream, #fffaf0); padding: 0.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1.5px solid var(--ink, #151b20);">
