      <!-- 8.3 THE SCORE MICROARCHITECTURAL ARCHETYPE METHODOLOGY -->
      <!-- ============================================================ -->
      <div id="archetype-methodology" class="schematic-container" style="margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <div class="schematic-header">
          <span class="schematic-tag">TAXONOMICAL RIGOR &amp; CLASSIFICATION METHODOLOGY</span>
          <h3 class="schematic-title">Why Archetype Counts Differ Across Domains: The SCORE Methodology</h3>
        </div>
        
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--ink, #151b20); margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
          A fundamental question in hardware evaluation is: <em>Why does SCORE classify 4 archetypes for Scalar Cores, 4 for Application Cores, 8 for Vector Coprocessors, and 5 for SoCs? Shouldn't a taxonomical framework force a uniform number (such as 6 or 8) across every domain for visual symmetry?</em>
        </p>

        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--ink, #151b20); margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
          The answer lies in <strong>methodological consistency over superficial symmetry</strong>. In SCORE, an <strong>Archetype</strong> is strictly defined by three universal microarchitectural qualification criteria derived from first principles:
        </p>

        <!-- 3 Universal Qualification Criteria Cards -->
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; width: 100%; gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
          <div style="background: var(--paper-deep, #eee4c8); border: 1.5px solid var(--ink, #151b20); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.15rem;">
            <span class="badge badge-yellow" style="font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 800; display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">CRITERION 1</span>
            <h4 style="font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 900; margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; color: var(--ink, #151b20);">Physical Phase Transition</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.5; color: #242c33; margin: 0;">
              Must represent a qualitative structural leap in datapath organization—<strong>not quantitative parameter tuning</strong>. Resizing an L1 cache or retiming a pipeline from 3 to 4 stages is parameter optimization; removing forwarding multiplexers or introducing dynamic register renaming is an archetype phase transition.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div style="background: var(--paper-deep, #eee4c8); border: 1.5px solid var(--ink, #151b20); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.15rem;">
            <span class="badge badge-teal" style="font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 800; display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">CRITERION 2</span>
            <h4 style="font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 900; margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; color: var(--ink, #151b20);">Governing Equation Shift</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.5; color: #242c33; margin: 0;">
              Must fundamentally alter which term in the Expanded Iron Law of Processor Performance dominates: trading cycles for area (CPI &gt;&gt; 1), eliminating memory stall bubbles (CPI<sub>memory</sub> &rarr; 0), or saturating memory bandwidth through spatial parallelism.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div style="background: var(--paper-deep, #eee4c8); border: 1.5px solid var(--ink, #151b20); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.15rem;">
            <span class="badge badge-coral" style="font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 800; display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">CRITERION 3</span>
            <h4 style="font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 900; margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; color: var(--ink, #151b20);">Subsystem Addition / Removal</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.5; color: #242c33; margin: 0;">
              Must introduce or remove a major architectural hardware entity: an Instruction Scoreboard, a Reorder Buffer (ROB), a Logarithmic TCDM Crossbar, a 2D Packet-Switched NoC Router, or a dedicated Vector Register File (VRF).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Master Archetype Summary Table Across Categories 1-4 -->
        <h4 style="font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 900; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; color: var(--ink, #151b20);">Master 21-Archetype Taxonomy Across Hardware Domains</h4>
        <div class="table-responsive" style="overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
          <table class="ds-table" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid var(--ink, #151b20); border-radius: 8px; width: 100%; font-size: 0.85rem;">
            <thead>
              <tr style="background: var(--paper-deep, #eee4c8); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink, #151b20);">
                <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.85rem; text-align: left;">Computing Domain</th>
                <th style="padding: 0.65rem 0.85rem; text-align: center;">Archetypes</th>
